{{NZelectorateShortDesc|current=yes|type=general|region=auckland}}'''Kaipara ki Mahurangi''' is an electorate to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. It was created for the {{NZ election link|2020}}.

==Population centres==
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Kaipara ki Mahurangi.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
The electorate consists of a large northern section of the [[Auckland Region]]. It stretches from the border with [[Northland Region|Northland]] to the northwestern end of [[Waitematā Harbour]], and consists mostly of various satellite towns north of [[Auckland]]. Population centres within the electorate include:<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.vote.nz/map/index.html?id=2&modified=2020110110227 |title=Map of electorates for the 2020 and 2023 electorates |date=17 April 2020 |access-date=18 April 2020}}</ref>
*[[Wellsford]]
*[[Warkworth, New Zealand|Warkworth]]
*[[Snells Beach]]
*[[Helensville]]
*[[Waimauku]]
*[[Huapai]]
*[[Kumeū]]
*[[Riverhead, New Zealand|Riverhead]]
*[[Muriwai]]

==History==
The electorate was created after rapid population growth in the former {{NZ electorate link|Rodney}} electorate caused the northern section to be carved off and incorporated into {{NZ electorate link|Helensville}}, which in turn lost the [[Waitakere Ranges]] to {{NZ electorate link|New Lynn}} and an area around [[Coatesville, New Zealand|Coatesville]] and [[Dairy Flat]] to {{NZ electorate link|Whangaparāoa}} (the former Rodney).<ref name="Commission">{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/assets/Boundary-Review/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2020.pdf |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2020 |date=17 April 2020 |access-date= 18 April 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Whyte |first=Anna |date=17 April 2020 |title=New electorate revealed, as raft of boundary changes announced prior to election 2020 |url=https://www.tvnz.co.nz/one-news/new-zealand/new-electorate-revealed-raft-boundary-changes-announced-prior-election-2020 |access-date=18 April 2020}}</ref> Initially, it was proposed to keep the name Helensville, but after opposition from residents in the Kowhai Coast area, that name was scrapped and it was named Kaipara ki Mahurangi instead.<ref name="Commission"/><ref>{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/boundary-review-2019-2020/view-submissions/ |title=Boundary Review 2019/20: View submissions |access-date=21 April 2020}}</ref>

In the 2025 boundary review, continued population growth in northern Auckland led to the transfer of Westgate and Whenuapai to {{NZ electorate link|Upper Harbour}}. An area west of Massey was shifted into the reinstated {{NZ electorate link|Henderson}} electorate, and a small area south of Coatesville was taken from {{NZ electorate link|Whangaparāoa}} and added to Kaipara ki Mahurangi,<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=17 September 2025 |page=18 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

The electorate draws its name from two areas of geographic importance, the [[Kaipara Harbour]], in the west of the electorate, and the [[Mahurangi River]], which is located on the eastern end of the constituency. Translated, the name essentially means Kaipara to Mahurangi, or Kaipara-Mahurangi.

